  "type": "2 marks — Identify Two",
  "marks": "1 mark for each clearly distinct legal duty, up to 2 marks. Acceptable answers include: obeying the law; paying taxes; serving on a jury when summoned; registering to vote (sometimes cited as a civic duty though enforcement varies). Two versions of the same point (e.g. 'following rules' and 'obeying the law') score only 1 mark."
 },
 {
  "text": "Explain why the rule of law is important in a democratic society.",
  "type": "4 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "Up to 4 marks. A single fully developed point with cause and consequence can score 3-4 marks. Two undeveloped points typically score 2 marks.\n\nAcceptable points include:\n- The rule of law means no one is above the law, including the government (point); this prevents those in power from acting arbitrarily or unfairly against individuals (developed reason); without it, rights could not be protected because governments could ignore legal obligations (consequence).\n- It ensures that citizens can challenge unlawful government actions in court (point); this protects individuals from abuses of state power (consequence).\n- It creates certainty and predictability in public life (point); people can plan their lives knowing what the law permits and prohibits (development)."
 },
 {
  "text": "Read the extract below and answer the question that follows.\n\nA fictional think tank published the following in a 2024 briefing paper: \"A survey of 1,500 adults aged 16-75 found that 71% agreed that democracy and the rule of law were important to them personally. However, only 38% said they felt the rule of law applied equally to all groups in society, regardless of wealth or background.\"\n\nWhat does the extract suggest about attitudes towards the rule of law in Britain? Use the extract and your own knowledge in your answer.",
  "type": "4 marks — Source Interpretation",
  "marks": "Up to 4 marks: 1 mark for accurately identifying what the source shows (a gap between support for the rule of law in principle and belief in its equal application in practice); 1 mark for interpreting the significance of the gap (the majority value the principle but do not believe it is fully achieved); 1 mark for using own knowledge to develop the point (e.g. referencing the Equality Act 2010, or debates about differential treatment in the justice system); 1 mark for a substantiated judgement about what this means for citizenship (e.g. that achieving equal application of the rule of law remains an ongoing democratic challenge, or that the gap between ideal and reality motivates civic action)."
 },
 {
  "text": "A local school is debating whether to add a module on British values to its personal, social and health education programme. Some parents and teachers support it; others are critical.\n\nDiscuss whether promoting British values in schools is the best way to build a cohesive society.",
  "type": "8 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Mastering (7-8 marks): A balanced two-sided discussion using connectives to weigh contrasting viewpoints. Names at least one specific piece of legislation or institution (e.g. the Equality Act 2010, the Human Rights Act 1998, the Association for Citizenship Teaching). Considers both the case for promoting shared civic values through education and the critiques of the label 'British values'. Reaches a substantiated overall position that clearly weighs the arguments rather than simply listing them.\n\nSecure (5-6 marks): Two viewpoints present and broadly accurate. At least one named real-world reference. Mostly balanced but the weighing of the two sides may be uneven. Connectives used.\n\nDeveloping (3-4 marks): Relevant points on at least one side, with limited development. One-sided or only a brief gesture to the other view. Little or no named example.\n\nEmerging (1-2 marks): Basic points about values or schools; descriptive rather than evaluative; no real-world example."
 },
 {
  "text": "A charity campaigning for civic education argues: 'Teaching the principles underpinning British society in schools is the single most important way to protect democracy.'\n\nEvaluate whether education about civic principles is the most important way to protect democracy in the UK.",
  "type": "12 marks — Evaluate",
  "marks": "Mastering (10-12 marks): Full two-sided argument. Arguments for might include: civic education develops informed, engaged citizens; countries with strong citizenship education programmes show higher political participation; the Human Rights Act 1998 and Equality Act 2010 need citizens who understand them to enforce them effectively. Arguments against might include: free and fair elections, an independent judiciary, a free press, and effective regulation of political donations also protect democracy; structural inequality means some citizens cannot exercise rights regardless of their knowledge; civic education alone cannot overcome low voter turnout or political disengagement rooted in economic inequality. Substantiated conclusion states which factor is most important and explains why, with reference to named examples.\n\nSecure (7-9 marks): Both sides present and mostly accurate. At least one named legislation or institution per side. Conclusion present but weighing may be limited.\n\nDeveloping (4-6 marks): Relevant points but one-sided or under-developed. Some real-world reference. Conclusion may be thin or missing.\n\nEmerging (1-3 marks): Basic statements about education or democracy. Largely descriptive with no substantiated judgement."
 }
]

