=== L3 Migration and Immigration ===
id: e786fbb3-8775-4877-b671-164081bb1ac7
slug: migration-and-immigration
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:55:05.685481+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Patterns of population change and the impact of migration on UK communities.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Migration?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="The movement of people from one place to another, either within a country (internal migration) or between countries (international migration).">Migration</dfn> is the movement of people from one place to another. It can be internal &mdash; within a country, such as people moving from rural areas to cities &mdash; or international, meaning movement across national borders. <dfn class="term" data-def="A person who moves to a country other than their country of birth or citizenship in order to settle there.">Immigrants</dfn> are people who move into a country, while <dfn class="term" data-def="People who leave their home country to settle elsewhere.">emigrants</dfn> are those who leave. The difference between the number arriving and the number leaving is called <dfn class="term" data-def="The difference between the number of people entering a country and the number leaving it in a given year.">net migration</dfn>. When more people arrive than leave, net migration is positive; when more leave than arrive, it is negative.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">A Brief History of Migration to the UK</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The UK has a long history as both a destination for migrants and a source of emigrants. In earlier centuries, waves of Huguenot refugees from France, Jewish communities fleeing persecution in Eastern Europe, and Irish workers seeking employment all contributed to the population. After the Second World War, the British government actively recruited workers from the Caribbean, South Asia and elsewhere to help rebuild the economy and staff the newly created NHS. The Windrush generation &mdash; named after the ship HMT Empire Windrush, which arrived at Tilbury in 1948 &mdash; became a defining part of British multicultural history.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">From the 1990s onwards, migration from European Union member states increased significantly, particularly after 2004 when eight Eastern European countries joined the EU and their citizens gained the right to live and work in the UK under EU free movement rules. Following the UK&rsquo;s departure from the EU in January 2020, freedom of movement between the UK and EU member states ended. A new points-based immigration system was introduced that applies equally to EU and non-EU citizens seeking to live and work in the UK.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: what ended in January 2020 between the UK and the EU, and what replaced it?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>When the UK left the EU in January 2020, freedom of movement between the UK and EU member states ended. A points-based immigration system now applies to both EU and non-EU citizens seeking to work in the UK.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Why Do People Migrate?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Geographers and social scientists use the terms &ldquo;push factors&rdquo; and &ldquo;pull factors&rdquo; to explain why people move. <dfn class="term" data-def="Factors that drive people to leave their home country or region, such as poverty, conflict, persecution or lack of opportunity.">Push factors</dfn> are conditions that make people want to leave a place &mdash; for example, conflict, poverty, political persecution, environmental disasters or lack of employment. <dfn class="term" data-def="Factors that attract people to a destination country, such as higher wages, political stability, family connections or better public services.">Pull factors</dfn> are conditions that attract people to a particular destination &mdash; such as higher wages, political safety, better public services, an established community of people from the same background, or family connections.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">It is important to distinguish between voluntary migration (people who choose to move, typically for economic or personal reasons) and forced migration. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A person who has been forced to flee their home country because of war, persecution or natural disaster and is seeking protection in another country.">refugee</dfn> is someone who has been forced to flee their country because of a well-founded fear of persecution on grounds such as race, religion, nationality, political opinion or membership of a particular social group. An <dfn class="term" data-def="A person who has applied for refugee status in another country and is waiting for a decision on whether they will be granted protection.">asylum seeker</dfn> is someone who has applied for refugee status but whose application has not yet been decided. Asylum seekers are not the same as illegal immigrants; applying for asylum is a legal right under international law, established by the 1951 UN Refugee Convention.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="Test yourself: explain in your own words the difference between a refugee and an asylum seeker, without looking at these notes.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>A refugee has been granted protection after fleeing persecution. An asylum seeker has applied for refugee status and is awaiting a decision. Applying for asylum is a legal right under the 1951 UN Refugee Convention.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Two viewpoints: What impact does immigration have on the UK?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Supporters of immigration argue that it brings significant economic, cultural and social benefits. Economically, migrants often fill skills shortages in key sectors: the NHS, social care, construction and agriculture all rely substantially on migrant workers. Many economists argue that migrants contribute more in taxes and public spending than they receive in services, particularly in their working years. Culturally, immigration has enriched British food, music, art, science and sport. Demographically, immigration helps offset an ageing population by bringing in younger workers who support public pension and healthcare systems.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n12">Critics of high levels of immigration argue that rapid population change can put pressure on public services such as schools, housing and GP surgeries, particularly in areas that receive the highest concentration of new arrivals. Some argue that unchecked immigration can suppress wages for low-paid workers in sectors where labour supply is abundant. Others raise concerns about integration: if migration is very rapid, they argue, it can be harder for communities to build the shared understanding and civic ties that make a diverse society function well. These concerns apply particularly to debates about policy, not to individuals or communities themselves. Examiners expect students to present both perspectives without dismissing either.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">The Impact of Migration on UK Communities</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Migration has shaped and continues to shape the UK in visible ways. Many areas of the UK have substantial communities with heritage from South Asia, the Caribbean, West Africa, Eastern Europe and the Middle East. These communities have built places of worship, businesses, cultural organisations and community networks that are part of local life. Languages, foods and festivals that originated elsewhere are now part of everyday British culture.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">At the same time, migration has also raised questions about housing, labour markets and public services in specific areas. Local authorities in areas of high migration have at times reported pressure on school places, social housing and healthcare. These are policy and resource questions, not inherent consequences of migration itself &mdash; they reflect whether government investment in public services has kept pace with population growth.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: Are refugees the same as economic migrants?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">Students sometimes use the terms &ldquo;refugee,&rdquo; &ldquo;asylum seeker&rdquo; and &ldquo;economic migrant&rdquo; interchangeably. These are legally distinct categories with different rights and obligations. An economic migrant moves voluntarily in search of better living standards. An asylum seeker is seeking legal protection because they claim to have fled persecution &mdash; their claim is assessed under international law. A refugee is someone whose claim has been accepted. Mixing up these terms in exam answers is penalised because it shows imprecise use of citizenship terminology. Use the correct term for the group you are discussing.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Migration and Citizenship Values</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Questions about migration sit at the intersection of several core citizenship values: human rights (including the right to seek asylum), national sovereignty (the right of a democratic state to control its own borders), equality (treating all people with dignity regardless of their origins) and social cohesion (building a shared civic society). How to balance these values is one of the most contested political debates in contemporary democracies. As a citizenship student, your task is to understand the different perspectives, use the correct terminology and present a balanced argument &mdash; not to take a personal political position.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Migration is the movement of people between places; push factors drive people to leave, while pull factors attract them to a destination. The UK has a long history of both immigration and emigration.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Refugees have fled persecution and are granted legal protection; asylum seekers have applied for refugee status and are awaiting a decision. Both are distinct from economic migrants and from illegal entry. Applying for asylum is a legal right under the 1951 UN Refugee Convention.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Immigration policy is a contested political debate: supporters highlight economic and cultural contributions, while critics raise concerns about public service pressures and integration. Students must present both viewpoints fairly and use precise citizenship terminology.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">Migration is one of the most politically contested topics in citizenship. On any extended-response question about immigration, make sure you present at least two clearly distinct viewpoints and that neither side is caricatured or dismissed. The most common error is producing an answer that reads as a personal opinion rather than a balanced analysis. For 8-mark and 12-mark questions, naming a specific law or agreement (such as the 1951 UN Refugee Convention, the Equality Act 2010, or the points-based immigration system introduced after 2020) will lift your answer into the Secure or Mastering band. For source interpretation questions on this topic, avoid simply summarising the source &mdash; reach a judgement about what it shows, link it to your own knowledge, and state what it tells us about the debate.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
