=== L5 The UK in the World: International Organisations ===
id: 7faf4288-4be3-4353-8251-09806f5fc01a
slug: the-uk-in-the-world-international-organisations
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:39:06.88951+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 26

--- DESCRIPTION ---
UK relationships with the UN, NATO, the Commonwealth, the Council of Europe and the WTO.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Does the UK Join International Organisations?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">No country can solve the world&rsquo;s biggest problems alone. Climate change, nuclear weapons, terrorism, and global trade all require nations to work together. <dfn class="term" data-def="Worldwide bodies that bring together member states to cooperate on shared issues such as security, trade or human rights.">International organisations</dfn> provide the forums, rules, and resources that make this cooperation possible. The United Kingdom is a member of several major bodies, each with a different focus and set of responsibilities.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall the five international organisations the UK belongs to, and one key purpose of each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The UK&rsquo;s five main international organisations are the United Nations (UN), the North Atlantic Treaty Organisation (NATO), the Commonwealth, the Council of Europe, and the World Trade Organisation (WTO). After leaving the European Union in January 2020, the UK is no longer an EU member but still participates in some associated arrangements.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">The United Nations</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The <dfn class="term" data-def="An international organisation founded in 1945 with 193 member states, aiming to maintain peace, promote human rights and coordinate responses to global challenges.">United Nations (UN)</dfn> was founded in 1945 after the Second World War, with the goal of preventing future conflicts. It has 193 member states and works through a number of key bodies. The Security Council handles threats to international peace and can authorise military action or sanctions. The General Assembly allows every member state one vote on major issues. Specialised agencies include the World Health Organisation (WHO), UNICEF (children), UNESCO (education and culture), and the World Food Programme.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">The UK is one of five permanent members of the Security Council, alongside the United States, China, France and Russia. This status gives the UK a <dfn class="term" data-def="The power held by each of the five permanent Security Council members to block any substantive resolution.">veto</dfn> over Security Council resolutions. Supporters argue this reflects the UK&rsquo;s global responsibilities and gives it disproportionate influence in maintaining world peace. Critics argue that the veto prevents timely action when permanent members have conflicting interests, as has been seen in several conflicts since 1945.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How the UN responds to humanitarian crises</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n7">When a conflict or natural disaster creates a humanitarian crisis, <dfn class="term" data-def="Non-governmental organisations that operate independently of governments to deliver humanitarian aid and advocate for those in need.">non-governmental organisations (NGOs)</dfn> such as Oxfam, Save the Children and Médecins Sans Frontières work alongside UN agencies to provide food, shelter, medical care and protection. The UN High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) coordinates responses for displaced people, while the UN Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs (OCHA) manages overall international relief efforts.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n8">The UK contributes to UN operations through both financial contributions and, at times, military personnel deployed on UN peacekeeping missions. These missions operate under strict rules of engagement: UN peacekeepers are authorised to use force only in self-defence or to protect civilians, and they must operate with the consent of the host nation where possible. The effectiveness of peacekeeping is a matter of ongoing debate &mdash; supporters point to reduced conflict in places such as Cyprus and the Democratic Republic of Congo; critics note cases where peacekeepers were unable to prevent atrocities, as in Rwanda in 1994.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">NATO</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A military alliance of 32 member states in North America and Europe, founded in 1949, committed to collective defence under the principle that an attack on one is an attack on all.">North Atlantic Treaty Organisation (NATO)</dfn> was founded in 1949 during the Cold War, primarily to provide a collective defence against the Soviet Union. The UK is a founding member. Article 5 of the NATO treaty states that an armed attack against one member is considered an attack against all &mdash; this principle of collective defence is the alliance&rsquo;s cornerstone.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Supporters of NATO argue that the alliance has prevented major war in Europe since 1949 and that collective defence deters aggression from hostile powers. Critics question whether the cost of maintaining NATO commitments &mdash; member states are expected to spend at least 2% of GDP on defence &mdash; is proportionate to the benefits, and whether the alliance&rsquo;s eastward expansion has contributed to international tensions. These are contested political arguments, and reasonable people hold different views.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">The Commonwealth</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A voluntary association of 56 member states, mostly former territories of the British Empire, cooperating on democracy, development and the rule of law.">Commonwealth</dfn> is a voluntary association of 56 countries, home to about 2.5 billion people. Most members were once part of the British Empire, though some have joined without a colonial connection to Britain. The Commonwealth promotes shared values including democracy, human rights, and the rule of law, and supports development through technical assistance and trade links.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Unlike the UN or NATO, the Commonwealth has no military or enforcement powers. Critics argue this limits its effectiveness. Supporters argue that its voluntary, cooperative nature makes it a positive model for international relations, particularly in helping smaller and developing member states access expertise and economic partnerships they could not obtain independently.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Write down the name, founding year and main purpose of each: the UN (1945), NATO (1949), the Commonwealth, the Council of Europe (1949) and the WTO (1995).">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The Council of Europe was founded in 1949 and is entirely separate from the European Union. It produced the European Convention on Human Rights (1950), which the UK helped draft. The Council of Europe now has 46 member states. The UK remains a full member, and the Human Rights Act 1998 incorporated the Convention into domestic UK law. This means Brexit did not remove ECHR rights from UK citizens.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">The Council of Europe and Human Rights</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">A common misconception is that the European Convention on Human Rights is an EU institution. It is not. The Council of Europe is a completely separate body from the EU, and the UK&rsquo;s membership of the Council of Europe was not affected by Brexit. The European Court of Human Rights, which sits in Strasbourg, enforces the Convention and can rule that a member state has violated a person&rsquo;s rights.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: does Brexit mean the ECHR no longer applies to the UK?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">This is one of the most common misconceptions in GCSE Citizenship. The European Union and the Council of Europe are different organisations. When the UK left the EU in January 2020 it left the EU&rsquo;s institutions (the European Parliament, the European Court of Justice, the single market, the customs union). It did not leave the Council of Europe, and the European Convention on Human Rights &mdash; enforced by the European Court of Human Rights in Strasbourg &mdash; still applies in full. The Human Rights Act 1998, which incorporated ECHR rights into UK domestic law, also remains in force. There is a separate ongoing political debate in the UK about whether to reform or replace the Human Rights Act, but at the time of writing the Act is still law.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n19">The World Trade Organisation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n20">The <dfn class="term" data-def="An international body founded in 1995 that sets the rules for global trade between nations and resolves trade disputes.">World Trade Organisation (WTO)</dfn> was established in 1995 to set the rules governing international trade. It provides a framework for negotiations between countries about tariffs, subsidies and market access, and it operates a dispute-resolution process when countries believe another nation is trading unfairly. The UK&rsquo;s departure from the EU&rsquo;s single market and customs union in 2020 means the UK now trades with the EU on terms agreed in the UK&ndash;EU Trade and Cooperation Agreement (TCA) rather than as an EU member. The UK also trades with non-EU countries either via bilateral trade agreements or under WTO rules.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n21">Supporters of the UK&rsquo;s departure from the EU argue that it gives the UK freedom to negotiate independent trade deals with countries around the world, including with fast-growing economies in Asia and the Americas. Critics argue that the UK&rsquo;s largest trading partner remains the EU, and that trading outside EU membership involves more administrative complexity and costs for businesses. Both of these are contested political positions and students should present them as such.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n23">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">The UK belongs to five major international organisations &mdash; the UN, NATO, the Commonwealth, the Council of Europe and the WTO &mdash; each serving a different purpose ranging from peacekeeping to trade governance.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Brexit in January 2020 took the UK out of the European Union but did not affect membership of NATO, the Commonwealth, the Council of Europe or the WTO. The European Convention on Human Rights still applies to the UK through the Human Rights Act 1998.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">Every international organisation involves trade-offs: membership brings cooperation, influence and resources, but also obligations, costs and shared sovereignty &mdash; arguments for and against must both be presented when evaluating their value.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n22">For extended-response questions about international organisations, the most common error is treating them all as the same type of body. Examiners want you to distinguish between them: the UN is universal and focuses on peace and human rights; NATO is a military alliance; the Commonwealth is a voluntary cultural and development network; the Council of Europe enforces human rights via the ECHR; the WTO governs trade. For a 12-mark Evaluate question, pick ONE organisation, present the case for UK membership (security, influence, cooperation) AND the case against or for reform (cost, sovereignty, effectiveness), then reach a substantiated judgement &mdash; &ldquo;On balance, UK membership of X is beneficial because&hellip; although critics raise valid concerns about&hellip;&rdquo;. Also avoid the ECHR/EU confusion: stating that Brexit removed ECHR protections is a factual error and will cost marks.</p>

