  "marks": "Mastering (10–12 marks): Full two-sided argument with named examples on each side. Engages directly with the concept of sovereignty. Reaches a substantiated conclusion that goes beyond 'it depends' — e.g. argues that the loss of sovereignty is outweighed by the security and influence gained, or vice versa, with supporting reasoning. May distinguish between different organisations (UN, NATO, Council of Europe, WTO) to show that the trade-off differs by body.\n\nSecure (7–9 marks): Both sides developed with at least one named real-world example. Concludes, but the judgement may be partially substantiated.\n\nDeveloping (4–6 marks): Relevant arguments on both sides but limited development OR one-sided. Some real-world reference.\n\nEmerging (1–3 marks): Basic points; little real-world example; largely descriptive.\n\nArguments supporting the statement: international organisations require member states to follow shared rules, potentially overriding national preference (e.g. WTO rules constrain unilateral trade policy; ECHR judgements have required UK law to change on issues such as prisoner voting rights); membership costs money that could be spent domestically; the Brexit referendum reflected public concern about sovereignty.\n\nArguments challenging the statement: sovereignty is exercised, not surrendered, through international cooperation — the UK voluntarily joins and can leave organisations; collective action delivers outcomes no single state could achieve (e.g. NATO deterrence, WHO pandemic coordination); the UK gains global influence disproportionate to its size through its UN Security Council seat; trade via the WTO framework benefits the UK economy. The Commonwealth and Council of Europe are explicitly voluntary with no enforcement over domestic policy beyond human rights.\n\nConclusion to credit: a judgement that weighs the sovereignty cost against the cooperation benefit with specific reference to at least one named organisation."
 }
]

