=== L6 Why Society Needs Laws ===
id: 35ef9ddc-712e-443c-82be-c40577c3b951
slug: why-society-needs-laws
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:27:55.917956+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
The principles underpinning law: presumption of innocence and equality before the law.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Would Society Look Like Without Laws?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Imagine a world with no rules about property, violence, or fairness. Without a system to settle disputes and protect people from harm, individuals would rely entirely on their own strength or the strength of those around them. Laws exist to prevent exactly this. <dfn class="term" data-def="Formal rules made by a recognised authority and enforced by the state, setting out what people may and may not do and providing consequences for those who break them.">Laws</dfn> are formal rules backed by the power of the state. They apply to everyone and provide a system for resolving disputes, protecting rights, and expressing society&rsquo;s shared values.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Rules and laws help society address complex problems of fairness, justice and discrimination. A law against workplace discrimination, for example, does not just protect one individual &mdash; it shapes how employers across the whole country behave. The same is true of laws around education, housing, health and safety, and the environment. Laws give individuals enforceable rights and hold both people and institutions to account.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write down the two fundamental principles of law from memory, with one sentence explaining what each means.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Two fundamental principles underpin the UK legal system. First, the <strong>presumption of innocence</strong>: every person accused of a crime is considered innocent until proven guilty beyond reasonable doubt. Second, <strong>equality before the law</strong>: the law applies to everyone in the same way, regardless of wealth, status or background.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">The Presumption of Innocence</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">The <dfn class="term" data-def="The legal principle that a person accused of a crime is assumed to be innocent until the prosecution proves guilt beyond reasonable doubt.">presumption of innocence</dfn> is one of the most important protections in a fair legal system. It means the burden of proof lies with the prosecution, not the defendant. In a criminal trial in England and Wales, the prosecution must prove that the accused committed the offence &ldquo;beyond reasonable doubt&rdquo; &mdash; a high standard designed to prevent wrongful convictions.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">This principle protects individuals from the power of the state. Without it, governments or powerful interests could imprison people simply by making an accusation. The presumption of innocence is recognised in Article 6 of the European Convention on Human Rights, which guarantees the right to a fair trial, and in the UN Universal Declaration of Human Rights (Article 11). It is also central to the common law tradition that England has developed over centuries.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why the standard of proof matters: beyond reasonable doubt vs balance of probabilities</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">Different legal proceedings use different standards of proof. In criminal cases, the prosecution must prove guilt <dfn class="term" data-def="The standard of proof required in criminal cases, meaning the evidence must be so strong that there is no reasonable alternative explanation.">beyond reasonable doubt</dfn> &mdash; this high bar protects defendants from wrongful conviction, since a criminal conviction can mean a prison sentence. In civil cases &mdash; disputes between individuals rather than state prosecutions &mdash; the standard is lower: the <dfn class="term" data-def="The standard of proof in civil law cases, meaning it must be more likely than not (over 50% probability) that the claim is true.">balance of probabilities</dfn>, meaning it is more likely than not that the claimant&rsquo;s account is correct. The difference reflects the much more serious consequences of criminal conviction compared to a civil remedy such as compensation.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Equality Before the Law</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><dfn class="term" data-def="The principle that the law applies equally to all people regardless of wealth, social status, ethnicity, gender or political position.">Equality before the law</dfn> means that no person stands above the law. A wealthy business owner charged with fraud faces the same legal process as anyone else. A government minister who breaks the law is subject to the same courts as a private citizen. This principle is part of the broader concept of the <dfn class="term" data-def="The principle that everyone, including the government and its officials, must obey the law, and that no one is above it.">rule of law</dfn> &mdash; the idea that government itself is bound by law, not just individuals.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">The rule of law is a cornerstone of democratic societies. It means that laws must be publicly known, applied consistently, and enforced by an independent judiciary. When courts are independent from government pressure, individuals can challenge state decisions. In the UK, the Supreme Court has ruled against government actions on several occasions, including in constitutional cases. This judicial independence is considered essential to equality before the law in practice, not just in theory.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Balancing rights and responsibilities: where rights conflict</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">Rights do not exist in isolation. In a diverse society, one person&rsquo;s rights can conflict with another&rsquo;s. A property owner&rsquo;s right to quiet enjoyment may conflict with a neighbour&rsquo;s right to hold a political demonstration outside. Freedom of expression can conflict with a person&rsquo;s right to privacy or protection from harassment. The law attempts to balance competing rights through legislation, court judgements and international agreements.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n13">The European Convention on Human Rights includes both absolute rights (which cannot be limited under any circumstances, such as the prohibition on torture) and qualified rights (which can be limited in specific circumstances to protect the rights of others or the wider public interest, such as freedom of movement or freedom of expression). Understanding where rights can be balanced against responsibilities is central to citizenship and to how courts make decisions in complex cases.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Why Society Needs Laws: a Broader Picture</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Laws serve several interconnected functions in modern society. They protect individuals from harm by others. They define the boundaries of acceptable behaviour in public and in private. They provide a mechanism for resolving disputes peacefully without resort to violence. They express the values that society has decided to uphold, such as prohibiting discrimination on grounds of race, gender or disability. And they enable complex economic and social activities &mdash; contracts, property rights, corporate structures &mdash; that would be impossible without a reliable legal framework that all parties can trust.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Test yourself: write down three different functions that laws serve in a modern society, giving one real example for each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Key pieces of legislation that have extended legal rights in the UK include the Representation of the People Act (which progressively widened voting rights), the Equal Pay Act 1970 (equal pay for men and women), the Race Relations Act 1976 (which outlawed racial discrimination in employment and housing), and the Human Rights Act 1998 (which incorporated European Convention rights into domestic law).</p>
</div>

<p data-narration-id="n17">Rights and responsibilities exist in local, national and global contexts. At a local level, planning laws govern what you can build on your property and protect your neighbours. At a national level, criminal and civil law set the framework for how disputes are resolved. At a global level, international agreements such as the UN Universal Declaration of Human Rights and the Geneva Conventions establish standards that countries are expected to meet. In all three contexts, rights and responsibilities must be balanced: a right to free speech, for example, comes with the responsibility not to use that speech to incite violence or hatred.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Laws exist to protect rights, resolve disputes, express shared values and enable social and economic life &mdash; without them, individuals would have no reliable protection against the power of others or the state.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">The presumption of innocence means a defendant is considered innocent until proven guilty beyond reasonable doubt; equality before the law means the same rules apply to everyone regardless of status or wealth.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Rights and responsibilities must be balanced at local, national and global levels &mdash; key legislation such as the Equal Pay Act 1970 and the Human Rights Act 1998 shows how the UK has progressively extended and protected citizens&rsquo; rights through law.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">For &lsquo;Discuss&rsquo; and &lsquo;Evaluate&rsquo; questions about law and rights, always distinguish between principles (the presumption of innocence, equality before the law, the rule of law) and how they work in practice. Top-band answers name a real piece of legislation or a real institution to show the principle in action &mdash; for example, the Human Rights Act 1998 as the way ECHR rights are enforced domestically, or the Supreme Court&rsquo;s independence as how equality before the law is protected. For a &lsquo;Discuss&rsquo; question asking whether the law always achieves fairness, you must present at least two viewpoints: one that supports the statement (examples of the law protecting individuals) and one that challenges it (examples where access to justice is unequal, such as the cost of legal representation). Use connectives like &lsquo;however&rsquo; and &lsquo;on the other hand&rsquo; to signal that you are weighing viewpoints, not just listing them. Avoid summarising the question back to the examiner &mdash; make a point, develop it, and link it back to the question.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
