=== L7 Criminal vs Civil Law ===
id: 8b77440c-c48a-443d-a484-704560aee375
slug: criminal-vs-civil-law
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:40:48.148313+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 21

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How criminal and civil law differ in purpose, parties, courts and outcomes.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Two Types of Law</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Not every legal dispute involves a crime. When a landlord fails to return a tenant&rsquo;s deposit, when an employer pays someone less than the law requires, or when a business delivers faulty goods, these situations are handled through <dfn class="term" data-def="The area of law that governs disputes between private individuals or organisations, where the aim is usually to provide a remedy such as compensation rather than to punish.">civil law</dfn>. By contrast, when someone commits an act that society has decided is harmful enough to deserve state punishment &mdash; theft, assault, fraud &mdash; this falls under <dfn class="term" data-def="The area of law where the state prosecutes individuals who have broken laws that society considers serious enough to warrant punishment.">criminal law</dfn>. Understanding the difference is fundamental to GCSE Citizenship and to everyday life.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write out the key differences between criminal and civil law: who brings the case, the standard of proof, the outcome, and the type of court used.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>In criminal law, the <strong>state</strong> prosecutes the accused. In civil law, a private individual or organisation (the <strong>claimant</strong>) brings a case against another (the <strong>defendant</strong>). Criminal cases must be proved <em>beyond reasonable doubt</em>; civil cases are decided on the <em>balance of probabilities</em>. Criminal outcomes include imprisonment or fines; civil outcomes typically involve compensation or an injunction.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Criminal Law in Detail</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Criminal law defines acts that are offences against society as a whole, not just against the immediate victim. When a burglar breaks into a house, the case is brought by the state (technically the Crown) against the accused &mdash; not by the householder personally. The <dfn class="term" data-def="In a criminal case, the legal process by which the state brings a case against a person accused of a crime.">prosecution</dfn> presents evidence to a court to show that the accused committed the offence. The accused person is the <dfn class="term" data-def="The person accused of committing a crime in a criminal case.">defendant</dfn>.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">The standard of proof is high: guilt must be established beyond reasonable doubt. This protects defendants from wrongful conviction. If found guilty, the court can impose a range of sentences, including a fine, community order, suspended sentence or custodial sentence (imprisonment). The aims of criminal sentencing are retribution, deterrence, public protection, rehabilitation and reparation. Different sentences may prioritise different aims depending on the offence and the offender&rsquo;s circumstances.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Types of criminal courts in England and Wales</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n7">Criminal cases are heard in different courts depending on severity. <dfn class="term" data-def="A lower criminal court in England and Wales where magistrates (justices of the peace) hear less serious criminal cases without a jury.">Magistrates&rsquo; courts</dfn> deal with summary offences and less serious either-way offences. Magistrates are unpaid volunteers advised on law by a legal clerk; they can impose fines and sentences of up to 12 months (or up to 6 months per offence for multiple counts). The vast majority of criminal cases &mdash; around 95% &mdash; are resolved in magistrates&rsquo; courts.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n8">More serious offences (indictable offences) are heard in the <dfn class="term" data-def="A higher criminal court in England and Wales where serious offences are tried before a judge and jury.">Crown Court</dfn>. Here, a judge presides over the trial and a jury of twelve members of the public decides the verdict. The Crown Court can impose unlimited sentences for the most serious offences. Appeals from the Crown Court go to the Court of Appeal (Criminal Division) and, in exceptional cases, to the Supreme Court.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Civil Law in Detail</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Civil law deals with disputes between private parties. The person who brings the case is the <dfn class="term" data-def="The person or organisation who brings a case in civil law, claiming that their legal rights have been harmed.">claimant</dfn> (formerly called the plaintiff). The person or organisation being sued is the defendant. The claimant must show, on the balance of probabilities, that the defendant is legally responsible for the harm caused. If the court finds in the claimant&rsquo;s favour, it may award <dfn class="term" data-def="Money paid by the losing party in a civil case to compensate the winning party for loss or harm suffered.">damages</dfn> (financial compensation) or grant an injunction (a court order requiring the defendant to stop a certain action).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Civil disputes cover a wide range of everyday situations: breach of contract (a company does not deliver goods as agreed), negligence (a driver injures a pedestrian through careless driving), defamation (someone publishes false statements that damage another&rsquo;s reputation), employment disputes (unfair dismissal, discrimination at work), and family law matters (divorce, child custody). The purpose of civil law is not punishment but remedy &mdash; putting the injured party back, as far as possible, in the position they were in before the harm occurred.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common misconception: is any wrongdoing automatically a crime?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">A very common error in GCSE Citizenship exams is to assume that any harmful or wrongful act is a crime. This is not the case. Whether an act is treated as criminal or civil depends on Parliament&rsquo;s decision about whether the harm is serious enough to merit state prosecution and punishment. A building company that cuts corners and builds a structurally unsafe wall may face a civil claim for negligence from the property owner &mdash; but the same act might also result in a criminal prosecution under Health and Safety law. The same act can sometimes trigger both criminal proceedings and a civil claim, though these are separate processes. The terms used in each system are also different: in criminal law you have a &ldquo;prosecution&rdquo;, a &ldquo;defendant&rdquo; and a &ldquo;conviction&rdquo;; in civil law you have a &ldquo;claim&rdquo;, a &ldquo;claimant&rdquo; and a &ldquo;judgment&rdquo;.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Civil Courts</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Most civil disputes in England and Wales are heard in the <dfn class="term" data-def="A civil court that handles the majority of civil disputes, including claims for compensation and contract disputes, with jurisdiction over cases up to £100,000.">County Court</dfn>. More complex or high-value claims go to the High Court. For claims under &pound;10,000, a simplified &ldquo;small claims track&rdquo; allows individuals to bring cases without needing a solicitor, though legal advice can still help. Employment disputes are heard in employment tribunals, which are specialist bodies designed to be more accessible than courts for resolving workplace disputes.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Tribunals and alternative dispute resolution (ADR) methods such as mediation and arbitration provide quicker, cheaper routes to resolving civil disputes without going to court. ADR encourages parties to reach an agreed settlement. Many contracts now include arbitration clauses requiring disputes to be resolved this way rather than through the courts.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Test yourself: name the correct term for each role. Who brings a civil claim? Who is accused in a criminal case? Which court hears 95% of criminal cases? Which court hears high-value civil disputes?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Three legal systems operate within the UK. England and Wales share one system based on common law and Parliamentary legislation. Scotland has its own distinct legal system (Scots law), with different terminology and courts including the Sheriff Court and the Court of Session. Northern Ireland has a separate legal system based on common law. The UK Supreme Court is the final court of appeal for civil cases from all three systems and for criminal cases from England, Wales and Northern Ireland (not Scotland, where the High Court of Justiciary is the final criminal appeal court).</p>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n18">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Criminal law involves the state prosecuting a defendant for an offence against society; the standard of proof is beyond reasonable doubt; outcomes include fines, community orders or imprisonment.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Civil law involves a claimant bringing a case against a defendant for a private dispute; the standard of proof is the balance of probabilities; outcomes typically include compensation (damages) or an injunction.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">The UK has three separate legal systems (England and Wales; Scotland; Northern Ireland), and a range of courts from magistrates&rsquo; courts and the Crown Court (criminal) to the County Court, High Court and employment tribunals (civil), with the UK Supreme Court as the final civil appeal court across all three systems.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n17">The criminal/civil distinction is tested frequently in short-answer and extended-response questions. The most common errors are: (1) using the wrong terminology &mdash; calling the person who brings a civil case the &ldquo;prosecutor&rdquo; when the correct term is &ldquo;claimant&rdquo;; (2) stating that both criminal and civil cases use &ldquo;beyond reasonable doubt&rdquo; &mdash; civil cases use the lower &ldquo;balance of probabilities&rdquo; standard; (3) failing to link the standard of proof to its justification &mdash; examiners reward answers that explain <em>why</em> criminal cases use a higher standard (the consequences of a wrongful conviction include imprisonment). For 8-mark Analyse questions, you will usually be asked to analyse advantages and disadvantages of one system, or to compare the two. Always name a specific court (magistrates&rsquo; court, Crown Court, County Court, employment tribunal) or a specific outcome (damages, injunction, imprisonment) to demonstrate precise knowledge and reach the higher bands.</p>

