=== L11 Crime, Punishment and Sentencing ===
id: 09174c3f-350c-4718-b60e-cd44818ea058
slug: crime-punishment-and-sentencing
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:20:59.74362+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 29

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Crime types, what shapes crime rates, sentencing aims and how the youth justice system works.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Crime?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A <dfn class="term" data-def="An act or omission that breaks a law made by the state and can result in prosecution and punishment.">crime</dfn> is an act or omission that breaks the law and for which the state can prosecute the offender. Crimes differ from civil wrongs: if someone breaks a contract, the dispute is resolved between private parties in civil court; if someone commits theft, the state acts as prosecutor on behalf of society. This distinction matters because the two systems use different standards of proof and different remedies.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Crimes are broadly categorised by their nature. <strong>Violent crime</strong> includes assault, robbery and murder. <strong>Property crime</strong> covers theft, burglary and criminal damage. <strong>White-collar crime</strong> involves fraud, embezzlement and financial deception, often committed by professionals in positions of trust. <strong>Organised crime</strong> refers to coordinated criminal enterprises such as drug trafficking, money laundering and people smuggling. <strong>Hate crime</strong> is any offence motivated by hostility towards a person&rsquo;s race, religion, disability, sexual orientation or transgender identity. <strong>Cybercrime</strong> &mdash; hacking, online fraud and identity theft &mdash; has grown rapidly as daily life has moved online.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, write down four distinct categories of crime with one example of each. Then check your list against the lesson.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Crime statistics in England and Wales are gathered from two main sources: police-recorded crime (offences reported to police) and the Crime Survey for England and Wales (CSEW), a large household survey that captures offences not reported to police. Together they give a fuller picture of crime levels than either source alone.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Factors That Affect Crime Rates</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">No single cause explains crime rates. Researchers and policy-makers point to a range of interconnected factors.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Economic factors:</strong> Supporters of the economic argument suggest that poverty, unemployment and inequality increase the incentive to commit property crime when legal routes to income are blocked. Critics point out that many crimes are committed by people who are not in poverty, and that correlation between deprivation and crime does not prove causation.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Social factors:</strong> Peer influence, family breakdown, and lack of positive role models are cited by some researchers as contributing to higher crime rates in some communities. Others argue this risks stigmatising communities rather than addressing structural inequalities.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><strong>Policing and detection:</strong> Higher levels of policing in certain areas lead to more recorded offences in those areas, which can make it appear that crime is concentrated there when the reality may be more complex. Conversely, underreporting &mdash; particularly of domestic violence and sexual offences &mdash; means official statistics undercount certain crime types significantly.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Social and situational prevention:</strong> Well-lit streets, CCTV, neighbourhood watch schemes and target-hardening (making property harder to steal) are examples of environmental strategies to reduce opportunity for crime. Education, youth clubs and employment programmes address longer-term causes.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common misconception: are crime rates always rising?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Media coverage often focuses on high-profile violent incidents, which can create the impression that crime is continuously rising. The CSEW, however, shows that overall crime in England and Wales has fallen significantly since a peak in the mid-1990s, despite increases in certain categories such as cybercrime and fraud. Understanding the difference between <em>recorded crime</em> (police figures) and <em>survey-based estimates</em> is essential for interpreting crime data accurately. Students should not assume that media prominence reflects statistical frequency.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Purposes of Sentencing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">When a court convicts a person, the judge must decide on a <dfn class="term" data-def="The punishment given by a court to someone found guilty of a criminal offence.">sentence</dfn>. The Criminal Justice Act 2003 sets out five recognised purposes that sentencing should serve. Understanding all five &mdash; and being able to apply them to specific offences &mdash; is essential for higher-mark answers.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14"><strong>Retribution</strong> means punishing the offender because they deserve it: the sentence is proportionate to the harm caused. <strong>Deterrence</strong> aims to discourage the offender (specific deterrence) and others in society (general deterrence) from committing similar crimes in future. <strong>Public protection</strong> removes the offender from society &mdash; typically through a custodial sentence &mdash; to prevent further harm. <strong>Rehabilitation</strong> seeks to change the offender&rsquo;s behaviour through education, treatment or skills training so they do not reoffend. <strong>Reparation</strong> involves the offender making amends to victims or the community, for example through community service or paying compensation.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write out all five aims of sentencing from memory. Against each one, write a sentence type that primarily reflects that aim.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The five aims of sentencing are: retribution, deterrence, public protection, rehabilitation and reparation. No single aim takes precedence in law &mdash; judges weigh them according to the nature of the offence and the offender&rsquo;s circumstances.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Types of Sentence</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">Courts have a range of sentences available. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A sentence that requires the offender to serve a period of time in prison.">custodial sentence</dfn> is reserved for the most serious offences. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A court order that requires an offender to carry out unpaid work or attend programmes in the community rather than going to prison.">community order</dfn> may include unpaid work, curfews, rehabilitation programmes or supervision. Fines are the most common sentence overall &mdash; the court sets a financial penalty proportionate to the offence and the offender&rsquo;s means. A conditional discharge means no immediate penalty but the offender is at risk of further punishment if they reoffend within a set period.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n18">The effectiveness of different sentences is debated. Supporters of custodial sentences argue they protect the public and deter serious offending. Critics point to high reoffending rates after release from prison, and argue that community sentences are more effective at rehabilitation and cost the public less. This is an area where evidence is contested and where extended-response questions often ask students to evaluate.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Two viewpoints on custodial sentences</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19"><strong>Supporters argue:</strong> Custodial sentences protect society by removing dangerous offenders from circulation. They serve as a strong deterrent, signalling to potential offenders that serious crimes will be met with serious consequences. For crimes involving significant harm to victims &mdash; serious violence, sexual offences &mdash; prison represents the proportionate retributive response that victims and the public expect.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n20"><strong>Critics argue:</strong> Prisons have high reoffending rates &mdash; around half of adults released from custody go on to commit further offences within one year. Short custodial sentences in particular are criticised for disrupting employment, housing and family ties without providing enough time for meaningful rehabilitation. Community sentences and treatment programmes have shown lower reoffending rates in some studies, at lower cost to the public.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n21">The Youth Justice System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n22">Children under ten cannot be prosecuted for a criminal offence in England and Wales &mdash; ten is the <dfn class="term" data-def="The minimum age at which a person can be held criminally responsible for their actions; ten years old in England and Wales.">age of criminal responsibility</dfn>. The youth justice system treats young people differently from adults, recognising that rehabilitation is typically more achievable and that the causes of offending in young people are often linked to their circumstances and development.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n23">Young offenders aged 10&ndash;17 are dealt with in the <strong>Youth Court</strong>, which is a specialist division of the magistrates&rsquo; court with restricted public access. Sentences available to the Youth Court include youth cautions (a formal warning), referral orders (attending a youth offender panel with parents), youth rehabilitation orders (community-based supervision and activities), and detention and training orders for the most serious cases.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n24">Youth Offending Teams (YOTs) coordinate the response to youth offending at a local level. They bring together workers from the police, probation, social services, health, education and housing to address the multiple factors behind a young person&rsquo;s offending. The aim is to prevent reoffending and support the young person into education, employment and stable housing.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n26">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n27">Crime covers many types &mdash; violent, property, white-collar, organised, hate and cybercrime &mdash; and is shaped by economic, social and environmental factors. Crime statistics come from two sources: police records and the CSEW household survey.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n28">The five aims of sentencing (retribution, deterrence, public protection, rehabilitation, reparation) are set out in the Criminal Justice Act 2003. The effectiveness of custodial versus community sentences is genuinely contested, and high-mark answers evaluate both sides.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n29">The youth justice system treats offenders aged 10&ndash;17 differently from adults, with the Youth Court, Youth Offending Teams and a strong emphasis on rehabilitation and preventing reoffending.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n25">The most common error on questions about sentencing is listing all five aims without applying them to the specific offence or type of sentence in the question. A strong answer ties each aim to a concrete example: for instance, <em>a long custodial sentence for a violent offence serves public protection because it removes the offender from society, and retribution because the length reflects the severity of the harm caused.</em> For an 8-mark Discuss question, remember that examiners want contrasting viewpoints &mdash; for example, the case for custodial sentences versus community orders &mdash; supported by at least one named example (such as referencing the Criminal Justice Act 2003 or Youth Offending Teams). Use connectives: <em>however, on the other hand, in contrast</em>. For a 12-mark Evaluate, your conclusion must add a substantiated judgement, not just restate the points. &ldquo;It depends on the aim&rdquo; on its own is not enough &mdash; follow it with <em>which aim matters most in this situation, and why.</em></p>

