+  {"lesson":2,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[2].marks","expect":"Up to 4 marks: 2 marks per way (1 for a basic point, 2 for a developed point with additional detail).\n\nAcceptable points include:\n- Ethnic diversity: the 2021 Census for England and Wales found around 18% of the population described themselves as belonging to an ethnic group other than White British; communities with heritage from South Asia, the Caribbean, Africa, East Asia and Eastern Europe all contribute to UK life.\n- Religious diversity: while Christianity is the most stated religion, significant communities practise Islam, Hinduism, Sikhism, Judaism and Buddhism; the proportion identifying as having no religion has grown substantially.\n- Linguistic diversity: over 300 languages are spoken in London alone, reflecting the range of migration patterns.\n- Diversity in age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity, all protected under the Equality Act 2010.","value":"Up to 4 marks: 2 marks per way (1 for a basic point, 2 for a developed point with additional detail).\n\nAcceptable points include:\n- Ethnic diversity: the 2021 Census for England and Wales found about 26% of the population described themselves as belonging to an ethnic group other than White British; communities with heritage from South Asia, the Caribbean, Africa, East Asia and Eastern Europe all contribute to UK life.\n- Religious diversity: while Christianity is the most stated religion, significant communities practise Islam, Hinduism, Sikhism, Judaism and Buddhism; the proportion identifying as having no religion has grown substantially.\n- Linguistic diversity: over 300 languages are spoken in London alone, reflecting the range of migration patterns.\n- Diversity in age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity, all protected under the Equality Act 2010.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F2: Correct the Census figure in the practice-answer guidance."},
