+[
+  {"lesson":1,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[2].definition","expect":"Respect for and tolerance of other people's rights, choices and beliefs, provided they do not harm others.","value":"Personal freedoms such as speech and movement, within limits set by law.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F1: The definition repeats mutual respect and tolerance instead of defining individual liberty."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"content_html","find":"around 18% of the population described themselves as belonging to an ethnic group other than White British.","replace":"about 26% of the population described themselves as belonging to an ethnic group other than White British.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F2: The ONS Census figure is 25.6%, rounded here to 26%."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[2].marks","expect":"Up to 4 marks: 2 marks per way (1 for a basic point, 2 for a developed point with additional detail).\n\nAcceptable points include:\n- Ethnic diversity: the 2021 Census for England and Wales found around 18% of the population described themselves as belonging to an ethnic group other than White British; communities with heritage from South Asia, the Caribbean, Africa, East Asia and Eastern Europe all contribute to UK life.\n- Religious diversity: while Christianity is the most stated religion, significant communities practise Islam, Hinduism, Sikhism, Judaism and Buddhism; the proportion identifying as having no religion has grown substantially.\n- Linguistic diversity: over 300 languages are spoken in London alone, reflecting the range of migration patterns.\n- Diversity in age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity, all protected under the Equality Act 2010.","value":"Up to 4 marks: 2 marks per way (1 for a basic point, 2 for a developed point with additional detail).\n\nAcceptable points include:\n- Ethnic diversity: the 2021 Census for England and Wales found about 26% of the population described themselves as belonging to an ethnic group other than White British; communities with heritage from South Asia, the Caribbean, Africa, East Asia and Eastern Europe all contribute to UK life.\n- Religious diversity: while Christianity is the most stated religion, significant communities practise Islam, Hinduism, Sikhism, Judaism and Buddhism; the proportion identifying as having no religion has grown substantially.\n- Linguistic diversity: over 300 languages are spoken in London alone, reflecting the range of migration patterns.\n- Diversity in age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity, all protected under the Equality Act 2010.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F2: Correct the Census figure in the practice-answer guidance."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"knowledge_checks","path":"[3].options[2]","expect":"18","value":"26","severity":"HIGH","note":"F2: The Census figure is about 26%, not 18%."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[0].a","expect":"Ireland (now Northern Ireland).","value":"Ireland.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F3: Ireland, not Northern Ireland, joined Great Britain in 1801."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"content_html","find":"Following the UK&rsquo;s departure from the EU in January 2020, freedom of movement between the UK and EU member states ended. A new points-based immigration system was introduced that applies equally to EU and non-EU citizens seeking to live and work in the UK.","replace":"The UK left the EU in January 2020. Freedom of movement ended on 31 December 2020, and the points-based immigration system began on 1 January 2021 for EU citizens moving to the UK.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: Free movement continued throughout the transition period."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"content_html","find":"When the UK left the EU in January 2020, freedom of movement between the UK and EU member states ended. A points-based immigration system now applies to both EU and non-EU citizens seeking to work in the UK.","replace":"The UK left the EU in January 2020. Freedom of movement ended on 31 December 2020, and the points-based immigration system began on 1 January 2021.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: Correct the end date and start date in the key fact."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"knowledge_checks","path":"[3].q","expect":"When the UK left the EU in January 2020, freedom of _____ between the UK and EU member states came to an end.","value":"Freedom of _____ between the UK and EU member states ended on 31 December 2020.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: The January 2020 date is the EU exit date, not the end of free movement."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[6].q","expect":"What changed about EU citizens' right to live in the UK after January 2020?","value":"What changed about EU citizens' right to live in the UK from 1 January 2021?","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: Free-movement rules changed after the transition period, not immediately in January 2020."},
+  {"lesson":5,"field":"content_html","find":"member states are expected to spend at least 2% of GDP on defence","replace":"NATO allies have committed to invest 5% of GDP annually in core defence and security-related spending by 2035","severity":"HIGH","note":"F6: The current NATO commitment is 5% by 2035."},
