search: your rights; PACE Code A 2023 (fetched 2026-09-04)","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F9","lesson":8,"field":"content_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"aged 18–70 who are registered to vote","truth":"In England and Wales, eligible jurors are aged 18 to 75 when their jury service begins.","evidence":"GOV.UK, Jury service: Who can be on a jury (fetched 2026-09-04)","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F10","lesson":8,"field":"content_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"custodial sentences of up to six months for a single offence","truth":"A magistrates’ court can impose up to six months for a summary offence and up to twelve months for an either-way offence.","evidence":"GOV.UK, Criminal courts: Magistrates’ courts (fetched 2026-09-04)","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F11","lesson":9,"field":"content_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"At age 16, a person in England, Wales and Northern Ireland may ... marry with parental consent","truth":"Since 27 February 2023, the minimum age of marriage in England and Wales is 18, even with parental consent. Scotland permits marriage at 16; Northern Ireland permits it at 16 with the required consent.","evidence":"GOV.UK, Legal age of marriage in England and Wales rises to 18; Scottish Government marriage consultation; nidirect marriage guidance (fetched 2026-09-04)","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F12","lesson":9,"field":"content_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"Westminster retains control over reserved areas including criminal justice policy in many instances.","truth":"Policing and justice are transferred matters on which the Northern Ireland Assembly can legislate. Westminster retains control of reserved and excepted matters, including immigration and national security.","evidence":"nidirect, The Northern Ireland Assembly (fetched 2026-09-04)","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F13","lesson":11,"field":"content_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"around half of adults released from custody go on to commit further offences within one year","truth":"For the April–June 2024 cohort, 67.1% of adults released after custodial sentences under 12 months committed a proven reoffence within a year; rates vary substantially by sentence length.","evidence":"Ministry of Justice, Proven reoffending statistics: April to June 2024 (fetched 2026-09-04)","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F14","lesson":12,"field":"content_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"the Geneva Conventions include ... the ban on using certain weapons such as chemical weapons and landmines","truth":"The Geneva Conventions and wider humanitarian law protect people in conflict. Chemical weapons and anti-personnel landmines are prohibited by separate weapons treaties.","evidence":"ICRC, International law on the conduct of hostilities: overview; ICRC, Weapons and disarmament (fetched 2026-09-04)","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F15","lesson":13,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"To become a magistrate, a person must apply to the Judicial Appointments Commission","truth":"The Judicial Appointments Commission does not recruit magistrates. Local Advisory Committees on Justices of the Peace assess candidates and recommend appointments.","evidence":"Judicial Appointments Commission, What we do; GOV.UK, Advisory Committees on Justices of the Peace (fetched 2026-09-04)","verdict":"FIX"}],"lessons_clean":[4,6,7,10],"summary":"Checked all 13 lessons against the assessment and subject-content sections of the specification and fetched official sources. Fourteen findings have surgical edits; one repeated asylum-law attribution needs a coordinated editorial decision."}

