+  {"lesson":8,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[3].marks","expect":"Up to 4 marks: identification (1), interpretation (1), own knowledge (1), substantiated judgement (1).\n- 1 mark for identifying what the source shows: a majority (62%) favour jury trial because they see juries as representative of the public.\n- 1 mark for interpreting a key detail: nearly three in ten (28%) are sceptical, suggesting significant minority concern about jurors' legal competence.\n- 1 mark for connecting to own knowledge: juries in England and Wales consist of twelve randomly selected registered voters aged 18-70 who must decide guilt to a criminal standard of beyond reasonable doubt.\n- 1 mark for a substantiated judgement: the source suggests majority public support for jury trials exists, but the level of disagreement (28%) indicates debate about whether lay decision-making produces consistent, legally informed verdicts.","value":"Up to 4 marks: identification (1), interpretation (1), own knowledge (1), substantiated judgement (1).\n- 1 mark for identifying what the source shows: a majority (62%) favour jury trial because they see juries as representative of the public.\n- 1 mark for interpreting a key detail: nearly three in ten (28%) are sceptical, suggesting significant minority concern about jurors' legal competence.\n- 1 mark for connecting to own knowledge: juries in England and Wales consist of twelve randomly selected registered voters aged 18-75 who must decide guilt to a criminal standard of beyond reasonable doubt.\n- 1 mark for a substantiated judgement: the source suggests majority public support for jury trials exists, but the level of disagreement (28%) indicates debate about whether lay decision-making produces consistent, legally informed verdicts.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F9: Correct the jury-service age range in the answer guidance."},
+  {"lesson":8,"field":"content_html","find":"Magistrates can impose fines, community sentences and custodial sentences of up to six months for a single offence (or twelve months for multiple offences).","replace":"Magistrates can impose fines and community sentences. They can impose up to six months&rsquo; custody for a summary offence and up to twelve months for an either-way offence.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F10: The custodial maximum depends on whether the offence is summary or either-way."},
