+[
+  {"lesson":1,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[2].definition","expect":"Respect for and tolerance of other people's rights, choices and beliefs, provided they do not harm others.","value":"Personal freedoms such as speech and movement, within limits set by law.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F1: The definition repeats mutual respect and tolerance instead of defining individual liberty."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"content_html","find":"around 18% of the population described themselves as belonging to an ethnic group other than White British.","replace":"about 26% of the population described themselves as belonging to an ethnic group other than White British.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F2: The ONS Census figure is 25.6%, rounded here to 26%."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[2].marks","expect":"Up to 4 marks: 2 marks per way (1 for a basic point, 2 for a developed point with additional detail).\n\nAcceptable points include:\n- Ethnic diversity: the 2021 Census for England and Wales found around 18% of the population described themselves as belonging to an ethnic group other than White British; communities with heritage from South Asia, the Caribbean, Africa, East Asia and Eastern Europe all contribute to UK life.\n- Religious diversity: while Christianity is the most stated religion, significant communities practise Islam, Hinduism, Sikhism, Judaism and Buddhism; the proportion identifying as having no religion has grown substantially.\n- Linguistic diversity: over 300 languages are spoken in London alone, reflecting the range of migration patterns.\n- Diversity in age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity, all protected under the Equality Act 2010.","value":"Up to 4 marks: 2 marks per way (1 for a basic point, 2 for a developed point with additional detail).\n\nAcceptable points include:\n- Ethnic diversity: the 2021 Census for England and Wales found about 26% of the population described themselves as belonging to an ethnic group other than White British; communities with heritage from South Asia, the Caribbean, Africa, East Asia and Eastern Europe all contribute to UK life.\n- Religious diversity: while Christianity is the most stated religion, significant communities practise Islam, Hinduism, Sikhism, Judaism and Buddhism; the proportion identifying as having no religion has grown substantially.\n- Linguistic diversity: over 300 languages are spoken in London alone, reflecting the range of migration patterns.\n- Diversity in age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity, all protected under the Equality Act 2010.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F2: Correct the Census figure in the practice-answer guidance."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"knowledge_checks","path":"[3].options[2]","expect":"18","value":"26","severity":"HIGH","note":"F2: The Census figure is about 26%, not 18%."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[0].a","expect":"Ireland (now Northern Ireland).","value":"Ireland.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F3: Ireland, not Northern Ireland, joined Great Britain in 1801."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"content_html","find":"Following the UK&rsquo;s departure from the EU in January 2020, freedom of movement between the UK and EU member states ended. A new points-based immigration system was introduced that applies equally to EU and non-EU citizens seeking to live and work in the UK.","replace":"The UK left the EU in January 2020. Freedom of movement ended on 31 December 2020, and the points-based immigration system began on 1 January 2021 for EU citizens moving to the UK.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: Free movement continued throughout the transition period."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"content_html","find":"When the UK left the EU in January 2020, freedom of movement between the UK and EU member states ended. A points-based immigration system now applies to both EU and non-EU citizens seeking to work in the UK.","replace":"The UK left the EU in January 2020. Freedom of movement ended on 31 December 2020, and the points-based immigration system began on 1 January 2021.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: Correct the end date and start date in the key fact."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"knowledge_checks","path":"[3].q","expect":"When the UK left the EU in January 2020, freedom of _____ between the UK and EU member states came to an end.","value":"Freedom of _____ between the UK and EU member states ended on 31 December 2020.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: The January 2020 date is the EU exit date, not the end of free movement."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[6].q","expect":"What changed about EU citizens' right to live in the UK after January 2020?","value":"What changed about EU citizens' right to live in the UK from 1 January 2021?","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: Free-movement rules changed after the transition period, not immediately in January 2020."},
+  {"lesson":5,"field":"content_html","find":"member states are expected to spend at least 2% of GDP on defence","replace":"NATO allies have committed to invest 5% of GDP annually in core defence and security-related spending by 2035","severity":"HIGH","note":"F6: The current NATO commitment is 5% by 2035."},
+  {"lesson":5,"field":"content_html","find":"When the UK left the EU in January 2020 it left the EU&rsquo;s institutions (the European Parliament, the European Court of Justice, the single market, the customs union).","replace":"The UK left the EU on 31 January 2020, but it remained in the single market and customs union until 31 December 2020.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F7: The single-market and customs-union exit followed the transition period."},
+  {"lesson":8,"field":"content_html","find":"Officers may stop and search a person if they have reasonable grounds to suspect the individual is carrying weapons, stolen goods or articles for use in crime.","replace":"Officers usually need reasonable grounds to suspect a person has weapons, stolen goods or articles for use in crime. In limited circumstances, a senior officer can authorise searches without individual suspicion.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F8: Authorised no-suspicion stop-and-search powers are an exception."},
+  {"lesson":8,"field":"content_html","find":"The police may stop and search only when they have reasonable grounds to suspect someone of carrying weapons, stolen goods or items for use in crime.","replace":"Police usually need reasonable grounds to suspect someone is carrying weapons, stolen goods or items for use in crime. In limited circumstances, a senior officer can authorise searches without individual suspicion.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F8: The word ‘only’ wrongly excludes authorised no-suspicion powers."},
+  {"lesson":8,"field":"content_html","find":"aged 18&ndash;70 who are registered to vote.","replace":"aged 18&ndash;75 who are registered to vote.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F9: The jury-service upper age limit is 75."},
+  {"lesson":8,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[3].marks","expect":"Up to 4 marks: identification (1), interpretation (1), own knowledge (1), substantiated judgement (1).\n- 1 mark for identifying what the source shows: a majority (62%) favour jury trial because they see juries as representative of the public.\n- 1 mark for interpreting a key detail: nearly three in ten (28%) are sceptical, suggesting significant minority concern about jurors' legal competence.\n- 1 mark for connecting to own knowledge: juries in England and Wales consist of twelve randomly selected registered voters aged 18-70 who must decide guilt to a criminal standard of beyond reasonable doubt.\n- 1 mark for a substantiated judgement: the source suggests majority public support for jury trials exists, but the level of disagreement (28%) indicates debate about whether lay decision-making produces consistent, legally informed verdicts.","value":"Up to 4 marks: identification (1), interpretation (1), own knowledge (1), substantiated judgement (1).\n- 1 mark for identifying what the source shows: a majority (62%) favour jury trial because they see juries as representative of the public.\n- 1 mark for interpreting a key detail: nearly three in ten (28%) are sceptical, suggesting significant minority concern about jurors' legal competence.\n- 1 mark for connecting to own knowledge: juries in England and Wales consist of twelve randomly selected registered voters aged 18-75 who must decide guilt to a criminal standard of beyond reasonable doubt.\n- 1 mark for a substantiated judgement: the source suggests majority public support for jury trials exists, but the level of disagreement (28%) indicates debate about whether lay decision-making produces consistent, legally informed verdicts.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F9: Correct the jury-service age range in the answer guidance."},
+  {"lesson":8,"field":"content_html","find":"Magistrates can impose fines, community sentences and custodial sentences of up to six months for a single offence (or twelve months for multiple offences).","replace":"Magistrates can impose fines and community sentences. They can impose up to six months&rsquo; custody for a summary offence and up to twelve months for an either-way offence.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F10: The custodial maximum depends on whether the offence is summary or either-way."},
+  {"lesson":8,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[10].a","expect":"Six months.","value":"Up to 12 months for an either-way offence; up to 6 months for a summary offence.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F10: The previous answer gave an incorrect universal maximum."},
+  {"lesson":9,"field":"content_html","find":"At age 16, a person in England, Wales and Northern Ireland may consent to medical treatment, marry with parental consent, leave school, work full-time and join the armed forces with parental consent.","replace":"At age 16, a person in England, Wales and Northern Ireland may consent to medical treatment, leave school, work full-time and join the armed forces with parental consent. Marriage law differs: 16-year-olds may marry in Scotland, 16- and 17-year-olds in Northern Ireland need consent, and the minimum age in England and Wales is 18.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F11: England and Wales raised the minimum marriage age to 18 in 2023."},
+  {"lesson":9,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[10].q","expect":"At what age can a person in England and Wales marry with parental consent?","value":"What is the minimum age to marry in England and Wales?","severity":"HIGH","note":"F11: Parental consent no longer permits marriage under 18 in England and Wales."},
+  {"lesson":9,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[10].a","expect":"16 (with parental consent in England and Wales).","value":"18.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F11: The correct minimum age in England and Wales is 18."},
+  {"lesson":9,"field":"content_html","find":"The Northern Ireland Assembly can legislate on devolved matters, while Westminster retains control over reserved areas including criminal justice policy in many instances.","replace":"The Northern Ireland Assembly can legislate on devolved matters, including policing and justice. Westminster retains control of reserved and excepted matters, such as immigration and national security.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F12: Policing and justice are transferred matters in Northern Ireland."},
+  {"lesson":11,"field":"content_html","find":"Prisons have high reoffending rates &mdash; around half of adults released from custody go on to commit further offences within one year.","replace":"Official data for the April&ndash;June 2024 cohort showed that 67.1% of adults released after sentences under 12 months committed a proven reoffence within a year.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F13: Replace an unsupported generalisation with the current official figure for short custodial sentences."},
+  {"lesson":12,"field":"content_html","find":"Key principles of the Geneva Conventions include: the prohibition on targeting civilians, the requirement to treat prisoners of war humanely, the protection of medical personnel and facilities, and the ban on using certain weapons such as chemical weapons and landmines.","replace":"Key rules of international humanitarian law include protecting civilians, treating prisoners of war humanely and protecting medical personnel and facilities. Separate treaties ban chemical weapons and anti-personnel landmines.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F14: The weapons bans are not provisions of the Geneva Conventions themselves."},
+  {"lesson":13,"field":"content_html","find":"To become a magistrate, a person must apply to the Judicial Appointments Commission and be assessed for qualities including good character, social awareness, sound judgement and the ability to see things from another&rsquo;s perspective.","replace":"To become a magistrate, a person applies through a local Advisory Committee on Justices of the Peace and is assessed for qualities including good character, social awareness, sound judgement and the ability to see things from another&rsquo;s perspective.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F15: The Judicial Appointments Commission does not recruit magistrates."}
+]
